## Recovered finding from the failed front agent-0f3a173 (08:52Z, never committed by it)

The front died mid-measurement with no durable output. The one thing it had established, before
dying, invalidates the *form* of any width table and is therefore the item's central constraint
from here on.

**The per-slot "affordable width" table is a per-slot MARGINAL, not a jointly affordable
allocation.** It is derived by holding every other slot at the uniform baseline
`affordable_opening_width` `b` and widening one slot, but the lattice is a **product** over slots:
the internal node count is `N(w) = 1 + sum over k of prod_{j<k} w_j`, not a sum over slots.
Widening every slot to its own marginal simultaneously therefore overruns the pop budget badly:

| depth | baseline `b` | per-slot marginal row | joint `N` | `pop_limit` | overrun |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 3 | 10 | 160/160/160 | 25,761 | 4,000 | 6.4x |
| 4 | 10 | 36/36/38/160 | 50,581 | 4,000 | 12.6x |
| 5 | 7 | 9/10/10/10/160 | 10,000 | 4,000 | **2.5x** |
| 6 | 5 | 5/5/5/5/5/160 | 3,906 | 4,000 | within |
| 7 | 3 | 10/11/11/11/11/14/160 | 2,210,791 | 4,000 | 552.7x |

The depth-5 row is the one that matters: 9/10/10/10/160 is the row the prior fronts quoted, and it
is 2.5x over budget. **A width allocation is admissible only if it satisfies the joint product
constraint, and an implementation that widens all slots to their marginals is inadmissible
regardless of what it does to the canonical case.** This is the same class of defect already
priced in [w-5b1e93](w-5b1e.md) (an inadmissible traversal bound) and it is the fence any width
change must clear.

Unrecovered from that front: the abandoned `MADGAB_PROBE_ONLY=187` rank measurement for the
canonical tuple, which the replacement front re-runs.

